<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Angoulême</h1><p class="st-meta">866 CE – 1308 CE (approx.) · capital Angoulême</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Medieval county of the Charente whose heiress Isabella married King John of England and whose lords included the Lusignans.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 866 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> appointed his kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vulgrin-i-count-of-angouleme">Vulgrin I</a> count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-perigord">Périgord</a> to defend the region against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids. His descendants, called the Taillefer line after the nickname of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-angouleme">Count William I Taillefer</a>, held Angoulême for more than three centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Aquitaine</a> and controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charente</a> valley, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a> on its fortified hill. The cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme-cathedral">Saint-Pierre</a>, rebuilt in the 12th century, shows the domed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanesque-architecture">Romanesque</a> style of the region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-of-angouleme">Isabella of Angoulême</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1200 and later Hugh X of Lusignan, whose family then held the county. On the death of the last Lusignan heir in 1308 it passed to the French crown. It was later an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois-angouleme">Valois-Angoulême</a> branch, which gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-angouleme.jpg" alt="County of Angoulême" width="320" height="308"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angoumois_in_France_(1789).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>866 CE – 1308 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Angoulême</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vulgrin I, William II Taillefer, Isabella of Angoulême</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Occitan, Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Angoumois</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1087&amp;focus=county-of-angouleme" aria-label="County of Angoulême on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4684 568 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Angoulême lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5004 824L5035 888M5004 824L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5035 888h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5004 824h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5004 824h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Angoulême. <a href="/atlas?year=1087&amp;focus=county-of-angouleme">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
